    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is structurally clean, additive, and idiomatic for this codebase.\n\nReasoning:\n\n- **Right unit, consistent placement.** `applyAddress` is added alongside the other static request-processing helpers (`normalizeTelephone`, `normalizeEmail`, `normalizeAddress`, `validatePostcode`) and directly beside `normalizeAddress`/`ADDRESS_ABBREVIATIONS`, which it delegates to. A maintainer would look for it exactly there.\n\n- **Coherent design that avoids scattering.** Rather than pushing \"structured-when-present-else-flat\" conditionals into every reader (household hash, postcode validation, locality), the implementation collapses the structured fields into the normalized/composed flat `address` at write time. Downstream readers stay untouched and there's a single source of truth for the stored address. That's the minimal, cohesive way to satisfy the spec's \"everything that reads the address uses the structured fields when present.\"\n\n- **No duplication introduced.** Normalization is reused via `normalizeAddress` for line1, line2, and the flat branch; nothing is re-implemented. (The pre-existing `REGION_POSTCODE_RANGES` duplication between controller and mapper is untouched and out of scope.)\n\n- **Not a catch-all.** `applyAddress` coordinates the three interrelated address fields (validate presence \u2192 normalize \u2192 compose) \u2014 a single cohesive responsibility. It reasonably takes the `Owner` and mutates in place (a slight departure from the pure `String normalize(String)` sibling idiom), but that is justified because it must set three fields together; a pure-return signature wouldn't fit. The embedded blank/absence validation mirrors what `normalizeAddress` already does.\n\n- **Create vs. update paths.** Create relies on MapStruct name-based auto-mapping to populate `addressLine1/2`, then calls `applyAddress`; update sets the three raw fields individually before `applyAddress`, which matches the existing field-by-field update style. Consistent with established patterns.\n\n- **Mechanical peripheral changes are uniform.** Model getters/setters, the four schema files, and the OpenAPI additions (including dropping `address` from `required`) are all straightforward and consistent with surrounding declarations, and the Javadoc documents the behavior thoroughly.",
